---
import Site from '../layouts/Site.astro';
import Icon from '../components/Icon.astro';
---

<Site
  title="A UI framework for large TypeScript apps"
  description="Build browser applications with JSX and Effect. Give developers and coding agents a consistent way to manage state, async work, and cleanup across features."
  section="home"
>
  <main id="main" class="landing">
    <section class="intro">
      <h1>A UI framework for large <em>TypeScript apps.</em></h1>
      <div class="intro-copy">
        <p>Build with JSX and Effect. Each feature defines its state, the work it starts, and when that work ends. Developers and coding agents can follow the same pattern across the application.</p>
        <div class="hero-actions">
          <a class="button primary" href="/docs/getting-started/">Get started <Icon name="arrow-right" /></a>
          <a class="button text-button" href="/docs/why-effectweb/">How EffectWeb works <Icon name="arrow-right" /></a>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>
    <section class="agent-example" aria-labelledby="agent-title">
      <div>
        <h2 id="agent-title">Feedback an agent can act on.</h2>
        <p>When an agent renders a list with JSX, the linter asks how to identify each row. Using the chat ID keeps focus on the same conversation when the list changes order.</p>
        <a class="inline-link" href="/docs/why-effectweb/#testing-with-coding-agents">How we tested coding agents <Icon name="arrow-right" size={14} /></a>
      </div>
      <div class="diagnostic">
        <p><a href="/docs/compiler/#diagnostic-codes">EW3001</a> <samp>Do not render JSX with rows.map(render).</samp></p>
        <div><span>Before</span><code>{'chats.map(chat => <li>{chat.title}</li>)'}</code></div>
        <div><span>Fix</span><code>{'list(entities(chats), chat => <li>{chat.title}</li>)'}</code></div>
      </div>
    </section>
  </main>
</Site>
